---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ESSAYS, HOW_PATH, SITE_LAB, adrUrl, essayUrl, howUrl } from '../../lib/links';

// The essay for sagas by alarm (docs/domain.md, "The principle map" and "CateringEvent";
// docs/architecture.md, "The catering saga"; Q33 · Q-principles). No lab page shows it, so the
// essay explains it itself and links the lab's index. Static, no island. Every claim about
// Cloudflare's behaviour is as its documentation stated it on the date in READ, and every number
// from the code names its file.
const READ = '2026-11-06';
const { title } = ESSAYS.sagas;
const SOURCES = [
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/alarms/',
    title: 'Alarms',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'one alarm at a time per object, a new one replacing the old, run at least once and retried with exponential backoff from 2 seconds, up to 6 retries',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/api/sqlite-storage-api/',
    title: 'SQLite-backed Durable Object Storage',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Durable Objects documentation',
    for: 'transactionSync() rolls back when its callback throws, and takes only synchronous storage operations',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://microservices.io/patterns/data/saga.html',
    title: 'Pattern: Saga',
    by: 'Chris Richardson, after his Microservices Patterns (2018)',
    for: 'a sequence of local transactions, choreographed by events or orchestrated from one place, with compensating transactions in place of a rollback and no isolation between sagas',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.enterpriseintegrationpatterns.com/patterns/messaging/ProcessManager.html',
    title: 'Process Manager',
    by: 'Gregor Hohpe and Bobby Woolf, Enterprise Integration Patterns (2003)',
    for: 'one central unit that keeps the state of a sequence and decides the next step from the results so far',
  },
  {
    href: SITE_LAB,
    title: 'The lab',
    by: 'ihaveasegway.com',
    for: 'five lab pages, none of them on sagas',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Proof of Concept Bakery`}
  description="How Proof of Concept Bakery runs a catering job that lasts weeks: one transaction per step in one object, timers behind a single alarm, compensations instead of rollbacks, and a 409 for any step out of order."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A catering job takes weeks and touches three objects and Stripe. No transaction can hold it.
      This is how the bakery runs it as a chain of small ones, how it undoes a step without rolling
      anything back, and why a step that comes in the wrong order is refused.
    </p>

    <h2>A breakfast for forty, over three weeks</h2>
    <p>
      An inquiry arrives for a breakfast spread for 40 people at a made-up venue, on a Saturday more
      than seven open days ahead. Staff send a quote. A few days later the customer accepts it and
      has 15 minutes of bakery time to pay a deposit of a quarter, or the day’s place goes back. Two
      days before the event a reminder is due. The kitchen bakes, a van delivers, and a balance
      invoice falls due seven days later. In the smoke test this job comes to 58,000 cents, deposit
      and balance together.
    </p>
    <p>
      Along the way the job holds one of the day’s two catering places in that day’s Schedule, takes
      two payments through Billing and Stripe, and has to notice time passing when nobody touches
      it.
    </p>

    <h2>What a simpler design does, and where it breaks</h2>
    <p>
      One transaction around the whole job would hold its locks for weeks, and no transaction spans
      two Durable Objects, let alone Stripe. A process that waits in memory for the next step is
      evicted long before the next step comes. A choreography where each object reacts to the
      others’ events works until something has to be undone, and then nobody owns the question of
      what “undone” means for this job.
    </p>
    <p>
      The 1987 paper “Sagas”, by Hector Garcia-Molina and Kenneth Salem, gives the shape that
      survives: a long transaction written as a sequence of short ones, each committed on its own,
      with a compensating transaction for each step that undoes its effect in business terms. No lab
      page shows this one yet, and{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        the lab
      </a>{' '}
      is where the family of techniques is explained.
    </p>

    <h2>One object, one step at a time</h2>
    <p>
      Each catering job is a <code>CateringEvent</code> object, one per inquiry. Its states are the
      twelve of <code>CateringStatus</code> in <code>crates/domain/src/catering.rs</code>, from{' '}
      <code>inquiry</code> through <code>quoted</code>, <code>accepted</code>,{' '}
      <code>confirmed</code>, <code>in_production</code>, <code>delivered</code>,{' '}
      <code>invoiced</code> and <code>paid</code> to <code>closed</code>, with <code>declined</code>
      , <code>expired</code> and <code>cancelled</code> as the other three ends. Every step is one
      command, decided against the state and committed with its events, its outbox rows and its key
      in one <code>transactionSync</code>. A step is started by a person (a route), by an event from
      the Queue, such as Billing’s <code>PaymentSucceeded</code>
      becoming <code>RecordDeposit</code>, or by a timer.
    </p>
    <p>
      The timers are a pure function of the state, <code>timers()</code> in the same file: the
      follow-up reminder and the quote’s expiry while quoted, the deposit’s lapse while accepted,
      the <code>event_soon</code> reminder while confirmed or in production, and{' '}
      <code>invoice_due</code> while invoiced. The object rewrites its <code>timers</code> rows from
      that list in the transaction of every change, so there are never more than two saga rows at
      once. Each row holds an id such as <code>quote-expiry</code>, a purpose, a wall time and a
      business time. Cloudflare’s documentation gives an object a single alarm at a time, so the
      object arms it for its earliest row, and when the clock is moved in the console every wall
      time is worked out again from its business time.
    </p>
    <p>
      An alarm runs at least once, the same documentation says, and is retried when it fails. So
      each timer runs its command under the key <code>timer:&lt;row&gt;:&lt;due&gt;</code>, and a
      second run finds the key and appends nothing. A timer that fires when its condition no longer
      holds, a lapse after the deposit was paid, emits nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      That makes the object the job’s process manager, in Hohpe and Woolf’s sense: one place that
      keeps the state of the sequence and decides the next step. Because it is a{' '}
      <a href={essayUrl('raft')}>single writer</a>, two steps never race inside it, and no other
      service has to coordinate them.
    </p>

    <h2>Undone by a step of its own</h2>
    <p>
      A saga cannot roll back what already committed in another object, so each failure has a
      compensation that is a new step. When the deposit does not come in time, the{' '}
      <code>deposit-lapse</code> row runs <code>LapseDeposit</code>: the job goes from accepted back
      to quoted, and <code>CateringDepositLapsed</code> tells the day’s Schedule to release the
      place. When a confirmed job is cancelled, <code>CateringCancelled</code> releases the place,
      and Billing refunds the deposit if the event was more than seven calendar days away.
    </p>
    <p>
      A compensation can itself be a call to another system that fails. A deposit that lands after
      the quote expired cannot confirm anything, so the object asks Billing to refund it in full at
      Stripe, under the delivery’s id and the reason <code>goodwill</code>. While Billing cannot be
      reached the object answers 503, the Queue delivers the event again, and the id makes the
      repeat ask for the same refund. The undo is retried until it lands, the way the step was.
    </p>
    <p>
      Some steps have no undo. Cancellation is taken from <code>confirmed</code> only, so once the
      kitchen has started the job runs to delivery and its invoice. No saga step voids an invoice:
      as built on {READ}, only the nightly wipe does, when Billing voids every open one at Stripe
      before it deletes itself.
    </p>

    <h2>Only forward</h2>
    <p>
      The log only grows. Going from accepted back to quoted is a new event, not the acceptance
      removed. A command that does not fit the current state is refused with 409 and the rule{' '}
      <code>out-of-order</code>, and nothing is written: a start of production before the deposit, a
      delivery before production, a cancellation after it. The bakery does not hold such a command
      for later, because a step that arrives early was usually sent from an old page. Repeating a
      step in the state it leads to is not an error and emits nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each of these refusals is proven over HTTP. On {READ} <code>scripts/smoke-catering.sh</code>{' '}
      passed 238 checks, among them the five 409s of decline, start of production, delivery,
      cancellation and staff cancellation, each from two states, each leaving the event byte for
      byte as it was (<code>docs/roadmap.md</code>, PR 7).
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>A timer row and an alarm run per timed step.</strong> Alarm runs that arm again are
        counted against <code>REARM_GUARD</code>, 100 a UTC day for one object (
        <code>workers/api/src/objects/stream.rs</code>); past it the object waits for its next
        command.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A 409 the client must handle.</strong> The catering pages read the rule and say
        “That step is not open in the event’s current state. Refresh to see where it is.” (
        <code>apps/web/src/lib/catering-errors.ts</code>), instead of trying again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No isolation.</strong> Between steps the job is visible half done: a place held with
        no deposit, a delivery with no invoice yet. Richardson lists this as the pattern’s price.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A quote you cannot watch expire.</strong> A quote is valid until the opening of the
        14th open day after it was sent, 16 calendar days or more, and the clock moves at most 14
        days (<code>MAX_OFFSET_S</code> in <code>crates/domain/src/clock.rs</code>). The expiry is
        proven on the seed’s quoted job only.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Sign in and send an inquiry from <a href="/catering/">the catering page</a>. Quote it in{' '}
      <a href="/app/">the console</a>, accept it, then advance the clock by 15 minutes before
      paying: the place is released and the job is quoted again. Accept again and pay the deposit
      with the test card 4242 4242 4242 4242. Start production, then try to cancel: 409{' '}
      <code>out-of-order</code>. Mark it delivered and pay the balance on the event’s page, and the
      console’s list of reminders shows each one the job fired.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      <li>
        <em>Sagas</em>, Hector Garcia-Molina and Kenneth Salem, Proceedings of the 1987 ACM SIGMOD
        Conference: a long-lived transaction as a sequence of transactions, each with a compensating
        transaction. Cited from print; not read on {READ}.
      </li>
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">
            {s.title}
          </a>
          , {s.by}, read on {READ}: {s.for}.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="hint">
      Back to <a href={howUrl('sagas')}>sagas by alarm on How it’s built</a>, to the decision that
      chose them,{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0004')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0004
      </a>
      , or to <a href={essayUrl('queue')}>how an event reaches the next object</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
